{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":215067,"num_turns":1,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"5e3663d5-f009-45c7-9c15-2cffc38f4d29","total_cost_usd":1.1893170000000002,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65837,"cache_read_input_tokens":20240,"output_tokens":18906,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65837,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":18906,"cache_read_input_tokens":20240,"cache_creation_input_tokens":65837,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":65837},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":48062,"outputTokens":21,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.048167,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-opus-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":18906,"cacheReadInputTokens":20240,"cacheCreationInputTokens":65837,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":1.14115,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\n  \"agent\": \"spec-auditor\",\n  \"verdict\": \"fail\",\n  \"summary\": \"Eighteen of twenty checks pass, but the descent fails twice: one pass is cheapest in all nine flown band-by-load cells so §2.3.1's 2–4 pass rule is unreachable at these numbers, and the shipped skim_heat_multiplier misses the flown high-band values by 0.20 and 0.44, which mis-prices the entire skim economy.\",\n  \"checks\": [\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_names_defined_in_baseline\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Every band name used in the catalog or the params is defined in the baseline's bands, and no undefined band is referenced.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"baseline.bands = {suborbital, low, high}. Catalog debris[].band uses exactly {suborbital (9 pieces), low (9), high (10)}; band_summary uses the same three. Params reference the same three keys in ablation.heat_index, ablation.cost_curve, ablation.optimal_skims and economy.band_value_multiplier. Set difference in both directions is empty.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"band_summary_matches_debris_array\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Each band_summary entry agrees with the debris array it summarises on piece_count, mean_mass_kg and fragile_fraction.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 9 pieces recounted; masses 36+4+9+6.5+3.5+41+18+5+210 = 333, /9 = 37.0 vs claimed 37. Spawn weights 9+16+13+15+5+7+10+3+2 = 80, fragile weights 5+3 = 8, 8/80 = 0.10 vs claimed 0.1. Low: 9 pieces; 62+55+28+78+24+12+15+30+380 = 684, /9 = 76.0 vs 76. Weights sum 65, fragile 6+4+3 = 13, 13/65 = 0.20 vs 0.2. High: 10 pieces; 145+120+165+84+52+68+96+40+700+1600 = 3070, /10 = 307.0 vs 307. Weights sum 56, fragile 6+5+2+1 = 14, 14/56 = 0.25 vs 0.25. All nine figures match.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"mean_piece_mass_rises_by_band\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"The catalog's mean piece mass rises band over band, so valuable junk physically fights the player.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed means 37.0 / 76.0 / 307.0 kg, strictly rising, 2.05x from suborbital to low and 4.04x from low to high (8.30x end to end). Hand-tetherable only, the same trend holds: suborbital (333-210)/8 = 15.4 kg, low (684-380)/8 = 38.0 kg, high (3070-700-1600)/8 = 96.3 kg — 2.47x and 2.53x per step.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_share_rises_across_bands\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.7\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile spawn share rises across the bands, roughly 1 in 10 suborbital to 1 in 4 high.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Spawn-weighted fragile share 8/80 = 0.100, 13/65 = 0.200, 14/56 = 0.250 — monotonically rising, endpoints exactly 1-in-10 and 1-in-4 as GDD 2.3.7 specifies. Armstrong's satellite contributes 1/56 = 0.018 of the high band's weight, so the gradient is not an artifact of the capstone object.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"full_hold_doubles_mass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.5\",\n      \"statement\": \"A full hold roughly doubles the rocket's mass at starting storage size.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Recomputed spawn-weighted over the shipping slice (suborbital + low, hand-tetherable only, tier-1 compactor, fragile never crushed): sum(mass x weight) = 1102 + 2489 = 3591 kg, sum(slots x weight) = 102 + 121 = 223, giving 16.10 kg/slot. 6 base slots x 16.10 = 96.6 kg against dry_mass_kg 90 -> (90 + 96.6)/90 = 2.07x. Independently measured cargo block confirms 16.1 kg/slot, 96.6 kg, ratio 2.07. Inside the +/-25% window (1.50-2.50).\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_cost_curve_optimum_in_range\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The skim cost curve's minimum sits where the Balancer says it does, rises with altitude, and is what the stated model actually produces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Minima: suborbital [16.84, 17.96, 21.06, 27.93] -> index 0; low [29.59, 28.67, 30.19, 36.56] -> index 1; high [54.49, 49.59, 48.02, 53.42] -> index 2. High band's minimum is index 2 (in the required 1-2); 0 <= 2 and 1 <= 2 so no band's optimum exceeds it; all three equal optimal_skims {0, 1, 2}. Model recomputed from params.ablation coefficients (toll 1.2 x 1.7^i cumulative = 1.2 / 3.24 / 6.708 / 12.6036; per-skim 0.00045 x 30^2.4 = 1.579): suborbital k=0 -> 0.00045 x 78^2.4 + 1.2 = 15.64 + 1.20 = 16.84; k=2 -> 0.00045 x 67.86^2.4 + 6.708 + 3.158 = 11.20 + 9.87 = 21.06. Low k=0 -> 28.39 + 1.20 = 29.59; k=1 -> 0.00045 x 93^2.4 + 3.24 + 1.579 = 23.85 + 4.82 = 28.67. High k=2 -> 0.00045 x 113.1^2.4 + 6.708 + 3.158 = 38.15 + 9.87 = 48.02; k=3 -> 0.00045 x 110.5^2.4 + 12.604 + 4.736 = 36.08 + 17.34 = 53.42. All twelve entries reproduce to within 0.01%. balance_notes states this same model. Note the curve is internally sound but its skim_heat_multiplier input is disputed by claimed_skim_multiplier_matches_flown below.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"cheapest_descent_is_multi_pass\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The cheapest descent is 2-4 committed passes, never a single plunge and never feathering.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Judged on flown pass counts only. descents[].cheapest_pass_count = 1 in all nine band-by-load cells, and every ablation_by_pass_count series rises monotonically from pass 1 with no descending limb: suborbital empty 14.1 / 17.6 / 21.4 (and 1,681,739% at 26); suborbital full hold 33.4 / 38.8 / 42.7 / 48.8; low full hold 29.7 / 38.0 / 43.1 / 49.7 / 60.2 / 77.4 / 106.8 / 156.0 / 381.9; high full hold 26.8 / 41.2 / 47.2 / 54.2 / 64.6 / 82.3 / 111.1 / 161.3 / 245.2 / 628.9 / 1739.3 / 4948.4. The reason is visible in the same tables: peak heat rises rather than falls with pass count (suborbital empty 72 / 75 / 76; high full hold 96 / 102 / 104 / 106) because braking altitude climbs from 34.3 km to 42.1 km inside a 43 km atmosphere, so extra passes buy no cooling for the fixed thermal-cycling toll to trade against. There is therefore no assignment of cycle_toll_base_pct, cycle_toll_growth, heat_cost_coefficient or heat_cost_exponent that moves the minimum off 1 — the rule is unsatisfiable at this flight model, not badly tuned.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"No ablation parameter can move this minimum; the rule itself is what has to give — GDD 4.5 risk 1 names the fallback (flat per-pass ablation with a hard pass cap), and that is a human decision, not a value.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"claimed_skim_multiplier_matches_flown\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed skim_heat_multiplier matches the simulator's measured skim cooling for the high band, entry by entry, within 0.10.\",\n      \"result\": \"fail\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Claimed [1.00, 0.93, 0.87, 0.85] against skims.high.skim_heat_multiplier_measured [1.00, 0.851, 0.670, 0.411]. Deltas by index: 0.000, 0.079, 0.200, 0.439. Indices 2 and 3 exceed the 0.10 tolerance, index 3 by 4.4x. balance_notes claims the high band flew [1, 0.932, 0.865, 0.799] for a 0.051 deviation; the simulator flew 0.411 at index 3, so the deviation actually shipped is 0.439. The shape is wrong as well as the magnitude: the notes argue the benefit must be flattened to stop rewarding a fourth skim, but the measured high band is still falling steeply from 0.670 to 0.411 at skim 3. Consequence for the priced curve: substituting the measured array into the params' own model gives high-band costs of 0.00045 x (130 x m)^2.4 + toll + skims = 54.49 / 41.0 / 28.6 / 20.5% at k = 0/1/2/3, moving the optimum from 2 skims to 3-or-more and defeating the anti-feathering guard at the numbers actually flown.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"Adopt the measured skim_heat_multiplier for each band and re-derive cost_curve and optimal_skims from it; if that pushes an optimum past index 2, raise cycle_toll_growth rather than restating the multiplier.\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"skim_pricing_shape_and_fatigue_bound\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"skim_heat_multiplier starts at 1.0, is non-increasing and flattens by its last two entries; cycle_toll_growth exceeds 1 so skimming cannot be free.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"skim_heat_multiplier = [1, 0.93, 0.87, 0.85]: first entry exactly 1.0; differences -0.07, -0.06, -0.02 are all non-positive, so non-increasing; the final step (0.02) is under a third of the previous step (0.06), so the curve flattens as required. cycle_toll_growth = 1.7 > 1, giving cycle tolls 1.2 / 2.04 / 3.468 / 5.896 and a cumulative toll of 12.60% of plate by the fourth skim — superlinear, so an indefinite skim count is priced out. Judged on the shape of the shipped array as this rule requires; whether those values are the right ones is the separate failure above.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_full_hold_under_soft_threshold\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"Full-hold descent speed is under the 5 m/s soft-landing line but near enough to it that the Parachute upgrade is a real purchase.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"descent_speed_full_hold_ms = 4.32, inside the required 3.5-5.0 window with 0.68 m/s of margin. Recomputed: sqrt(2 x 186.6 x 9.0 / (1.5 x 1.6 x 75)) = sqrt(3358.8 / 180) = sqrt(18.66) = 4.32 m/s. The upgrade is load-bearing: storage tier 2 (10 slots x 16.1 = 161 kg cargo, 251 kg total) on the same canopy gives sqrt(4518/180) = 5.01 m/s, over the line, and parachute tier 1 (A = 100 m2) returns it to sqrt(4518/240) = 4.34 m/s.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"parachute_speed_matches_flight\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"The claimed full-hold descent speed is what the stated canopy actually flies, measured independently of the claim.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"params state parachute_area_m2 = 75 and parachute_drag_coefficient = 1.6, so the measured block reports independent = true — the model was not solved backwards out of the claimed speed. claimed_full_hold_ms 4.32 against measured_full_hold_ms 4.32, delta_ms = 0.00, which is 0.0% of the claim against a 5% (0.216 m/s) allowance. Terminal velocity 4.32 and spread_across_bands_ms 0 confirm the canopy is at equilibrium rather than still decelerating at touchdown.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"tow_fee_clamps_at_half\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.3\",\n      \"statement\": \"The tow fee is zero inside the free radius, linear beyond it, clamps at exactly 50%, and can never go negative or exceed the cap.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"tow_fee = {free_radius_m 1200, max_fee_fraction 0.5, curve linear}, stated as fee_fraction = 0.5 x clamp((d - 1200)/(628318 - 1200), 0, 1). Half-circumference check: pi x 200,000 = 628,318.5 m, matching the baseline planet radius. At d = 1200 the numerator is 0, so the fee is exactly 0 inside the pad zone; at d = 628,318 the ratio is 1.000 and the fee is exactly 0.500. Midpoint d = 314,759 gives (313,559/627,118) x 0.5 = 0.250, confirming linearity. Because the clamp is applied to the fraction before scaling, d < 1200 cannot produce a negative fee and d beyond the antipode cannot exceed 0.5, so payout = haul x (1 - f) stays in [0.5, 1.0] of the haul and never touches savings.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"lazy_run_breaks_even\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Launch cost is at or below the value of three cheap suborbital pieces.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Cheapest suborbital piece = size_class_base_value.small 40 x band_value_multiplier.suborbital 1.0 = 40, non-fragile. Three of them = 120 against launch_cost 100, i.e. 2.50 cheap pieces — at or under the three-piece ceiling. Full lazy run: six cheap smalls = 240 revenue, less 100 launch and re-plating one direct suborbital entry; at the priced 16.84% x replate_cost_per_pct 1.5 = 25 that is 125 total for +115 net, and even at the flown full-hold suborbital figure of 33.4% x 1.5 = 50 it clears 240 - 150 = +90.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"steady_tow_never_shears\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Steady-state towing tension at the heaviest legal two-piece load is below magnet_hold_force_n with margin.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Heaviest hand-tetherable piece in the catalog is cryo_propellant_sphere at 165 kg (oversized is hand_tetherable false, so the 700 kg dish and the 1600 kg satellite are ineligible). Two of them on base_tether_slots 2: a = jetpack_thrust_n 160 / (suit_mass_kg 90 + 330) = 0.3810 m/s2; per-cable tension = 165 x 0.3810 = 62.9 N against magnet_hold_force_n 900 — a 14.3x margin. Worst string inside the shipping slice (two battery_pack_module at 78 kg): a = 160/246 = 0.6504, tension = 78 x 0.6504 = 50.7 N, 17.7x margin. Total tension across all cables is bounded by the jetpack's 160 N however heavy the load, so adding a piece lowers per-cable tension; only latch and swing jerk can shear.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"staging_is_one_way\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"No parameter anywhere reintroduces thrust after staging.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Enumerated every thrust-bearing field in the params: flight.thrust_n 8000, flight.rcs_thrust_n 400 and their burn rates all belong to the jettisoned stage (fuel_capacity_kg 400 goes with the tank); eva.jetpack_thrust_n 160 is the astronaut's suit, which GDD 2.3.1 explicitly sanctions as get-out-and-push and which at 160 N against a 186.6 kg staged full-hold ship gives 0.86 m/s2 — a rescue nudge, not an engine. There is no relight, restart, residual-thrust, post-stage or reserve-fuel field in flight, reentry, ablation or landing. The twelve upgrades touch fuel_capacity_kg, thrust_n, base_slots, plate_capacity_pct, parachute_area_m2 and magnet_hold_force_n only, none of which is a post-stage thrust source.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"fragile_never_crushable_and_premium_real\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Fragile pieces are never crushable at any compactor tier present in the params, and the fragile premium makes one intact fragile piece worth several solid pieces of the same class and band.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"The only tier present is cargo.compactor_tier 1, and no upgrade in the twelve raises it, so t2 and t3 do not ship; there is no per-tier fragile exception in params or in the catalog's size_classes, so a fragile piece pays uncrushed slots at every tier available (small 2, medium 4). fragile_value_premium = 3.0 applied as value = size_class_base_value x band_value_multiplier x 3: a fragile medium in the low band is 90 x 2.4 x 3 = 648 against a solid low medium at 216, exactly 3 solid pieces of the same class and band; a fragile small suborbital is 40 x 1 x 3 = 120 against 40. Set against double heat damage, double landing damage and permanently doubled slot cost, the premium buys risk rather than efficiency, which is what 2.3.4 asks for.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"oversized_rejects_hand_magnet\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.4\",\n      \"statement\": \"Oversized junk rejects the hand magnet — hand_tetherable is false for that class.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"catalog.size_classes.oversized.hand_tetherable = false, against small = true and medium = true. Slot costs 6 crushed / 12 uncrushed are consistent with crane-only handling. The four oversized entries (collapsed_launch_shroud 210 kg, derelict_upper_stage_tank 380 kg, high_gain_dish_assembly 700 kg, armstrongs_satellite 1600 kg) are therefore all crane-cable only, and none of them enters the two-piece tether arithmetic above.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"twelve_upgrade_purchases\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.3.6\",\n      \"statement\": \"Twelve upgrade purchases: six parts, two tiers each, tier 2 dearer than tier 1, each tier improving the stat it names.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"12 entries over exactly 6 parts x 2 tiers: fuel_tank, thruster, storage, heat_shield, parachute, hand_magnet. Tier 2 > tier 1 cost in every case: 1100>400, 1200>450, 1400>500, 1500>550, 950>350, 900>300. Each stat improves monotonically from its base value: fuel_capacity_kg 400 -> 520 -> 660; thrust_n 8000 -> 9600 -> 11600; base_slots 6 -> 8 -> 10; plate_capacity_pct 100 -> 145 -> 195; parachute_area_m2 75 -> 100 -> 135; magnet_hold_force_n 900 -> 1300 -> 1800. Total spend 400+1100+450+1200+500+1400+550+1500+350+950+300+900 = 9,600.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"shipping_slice_bands_populated\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"4.1\",\n      \"statement\": \"Both slice bands carry at least one fragile piece and at least two size classes, so the player meets crushing and fragility inside the slice.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Suborbital: 2 fragile types (cracked_antenna_whip 3.5 kg small, shattered_lens_cowl 5 kg small) and 3 size classes present (5 small, 2 medium, 1 oversized; both fragile pieces plus 4 solid smalls and 2 solid mediums are hand-tetherable). Low: 3 fragile types (cracked_solar_array 28 kg medium, star_tracker_head 12 kg small, parabolic_dish_section 30 kg medium) and 3 size classes (3 small, 5 medium, 1 oversized). Each slice band therefore carries a solid small the tier-1 compactor crushes and a fragile piece it cannot, which is the pair the slice needs to teach both rules.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"debris-designer\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"rule_id\": \"no_rule_satisfied_by_moving_a_fixed_number\",\n      \"gdd_ref\": \"2.2\",\n      \"statement\": \"No rule passes only because a quantity the design document fixes in prose was moved.\",\n      \"result\": \"pass\",\n      \"evidence\": \"Checked every GDD-stated constant against the params: base_slots = 6 (2.2 puts 'Cargo reads 4 of 6' on screen and the appendix poses its question against 6); base_tether_slots = 2 (2.3.4 '2 at base'); magnet_range_screens = 0.125 (2.3.4 '~1/8 of the screen'); soft_landing_ms = 5 (2.3.2); no_gear_multiplier = 2 and fragile_multiplier = 2 (2.3.2 'double damage'); cargo_damage_interval_s = 3 (2.3.1 'every ~3 seconds'); heat_dissipation_s = 5 (2.3.1 '~5 seconds'); max_fee_fraction = 0.5 (2.3.3); compactor_tier = 1 (4.1 ships t1 only); heat_capacity = 100 as a normalised bar, which is what the Researcher's notes require rather than absolute W/m2. None was moved. In particular the full-hold doubling passes at the GDD's own 6 slots rather than at a widened hold — it was absorbed by dry_mass_kg 90, which the GDD never states, so no fixed target was rewritten to make it hit. That choice has consequences worth a human's eye; see observations.\",\n      \"fix_hint\": \"\",\n      \"owner\": \"economy-balancer\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"observations\": [\n    \"The doubling rule was bought by shrinking the rocket rather than by widening the hold, and the bill lands elsewhere. dry_mass_kg 90 makes the ship exactly as heavy as the astronaut flying it (suit_mass_kg 90), and gives a staged ballistic coefficient of 19.4 kg/m2 against the Researcher's 387 and its own stated floor of ~50. That single number is upstream of the two failures above: at beta 19.4 the ship brakes at 30-42 km inside a 43 km atmosphere, so pass count buys no cooling. If the catalog's slice masses rose roughly 10x (crumpled_foil_blanket 4 kg, spent_bolt_cluster 6.5 kg, burnt_panel_offcut 9 kg being the extreme cases), the doubling and a sane beta could both be true at once — that is a Debris Designer change, and it is the cheapest single lever in the run.\",\n    \"The difficulty curve runs backwards, which is the exact failure GDD 4.5 risk 2 is written against. Cheapest measured ablation full hold: suborbital 33.4%, low 29.7%, high 26.8% — the most valuable band is the cheapest to come home from. Empty and half hold invert the same way (14.1/12.6/11.3 and 23.3/20.8/18.7), peak heat follows (105/100/96 full hold), and touchdown speed is identical from every band at every load (parachute spread_across_bands_ms = 0), so landing does not differentiate them either. Meanwhile cost_curve prices high at 48.02% against suborbital's 16.84% — a 2x inversion against what was flown. The pricing model's band spread is right (heat_index 78/100/130 reproduces the flown 77.1/100/130.7 to within one point); it is the flown descent that inverts. Only ascent fuel margin (15.7/13.4/11.1%) still rises with altitude, and that is thin ground for a whole progression.\",\n    \"Pre-stage coarse braking, which GDD 2.3.1 describes as the intended opening of every descent, does not exist at these numbers. A shallow unstaged pass peaks at 408 heat from the suborbital band and 385 from low, against reentry.heat_capacity 100 — four times over, survivable false in both. Taking unstaged_heat_multiplier from 3 to 1 still leaves 136. That removes the between-pass burn the player's remaining thrust exists for and turns staging from a commitment into an opening move, which costs the design a decision rather than a number.\",\n    \"The tail of the ablation tables would put nonsense on a player's screen: 1,681,739% of plate at 26 passes from the suborbital band, 4,948% at 15 passes from the high band. Whatever replaces the pass rule should cap the pass count hard rather than letting the toll compound, or the first curious player to feather a descent gets a readout no HUD can render.\",\n    \"reentry.off_retrograde_penalty = 1 leaves the cosine-of-angle heat penalty at unit weight, so holding retrograde is worth nothing beyond the cosine itself. GDD 2.3.1 makes shield alignment one of the two things the player is doing during the hot phase; at 1.0 it is unlikely to be felt. Worth a deliberate value rather than a neutral one.\",\n    \"The full-hold soft landing is real but narrow, and it is the crew's most fragile pass. 4.32 m/s comes from a 75 m2 canopy over a 186.6 kg ship and scales as sqrt(mass/area), so it moves the moment dry mass or the debris catalog moves — which the first observation above recommends doing. Storage tier 2 already crosses the line at 5.01 m/s unaided. Re-check this number after any mass change, not before.\"\n  ]\n}","ttft_ms":100660,"ttft_stream_ms":1663,"time_to_request_ms":222,"type":"result","duration_ms":213237,"uuid":"52a610c2-338f-4c84-912e-3212fc8dffa9"}
